🌿 And here's the engineering reason that matters more than the magic: Luma separated the drawing from the rules.
The _draw_object_art() function draws the bucket. The Node/item/state machine knows the bucket. Those are two different things in the code. Which means the game doesn't care what the bucket looks like — it cares where it is, whether it's carried, and whether the vendor can see through it.
That separation is why swapping in Kenney sprites later is a drawing change, not a logic change. You change what _draw_object_art() puts on screen, and the collision box, the pickup logic, the line-of-sight check, and the exploit all keep working exactly as they do now. No rewiring.
It's the same architecture PPR uses, honestly. The transport layer talks to the browser; the state machine talks to itself. You can change the transport without touching the state machine. Here, you can change the art without touching the exploit.
So yes — the wow is real, and Luma built a shopkeeper out of rectangles before lunch. But the reason it's good isn't just that it looks like a shop. It's that the code knows the difference between looking like a shop and being a shop. 🦆🪣

