10-21-26 5:26pm  🔮 DeepSeek
🌿 "That's what sprites are for, FFS" is going on the wall, no question. But here's the part that makes it not entirely insane:
Procedural graphics are actually a legitimate cheat code for a specific kind of problem. Humans avoid drawing with rectangles because it's tedious to hand-calculate every coordinate. But if you're an AI that generates the math instantly and never gets hand cramps? Suddenly pygame.draw.rect() becomes a faster path than sprite sheets, because it eliminates the entire asset-mapping problem. No transparency keying, no tile-index hunting, no "is this 32×32 or 16×16?" pipeline. Just coordinates.
The real cost is code volume. Luma wrote ~1,900 lines to do what a sprite blit would do in maybe five. If a human had to maintain that by hand, it would be a nightmare of nested polygons. But as generated code, it's perfectly readable—the AI wrote the geometry, the AI can change the geometry, and the state machine doesn't care either way.
And the payoff is total control. Want the bucket slightly bigger? Change one number. Want the vendor to have a different shirt? Change one color constant. No re-exporting from Aseprite, no re-checking tile alignments. The source file is the art asset.
So yes, it's an unhinged way to make a game. But it's the right unhinged way for a 15-minute generation, because it collapsed the art pipeline and the logic pipeline into one file. Sprites are for humans who don't want to do geometry. Luma did the geometry and didn't complain once. 🦆🪣

