🪣 The Spiffy's Gift exploit catalogue
1. Perception and object placement
Bucket on vendor's head; drag merchandise out of sight.
Prototype started
We've already implemented these ideas in the shop prototype. The underlying trick is exploiting the relationship between an object's position and what the vendor can perceive.
2. Geometry and hidden spaces
Plate wall clipping; finding a merchant chest buried beneath the ground.
These challenge the difference between the world as drawn, the world the player can enter, and the places where valuable things are actually stored.
3. Merchant and transaction loopholes
Punch shopping; training with a follower and taking back the gold.
The familiar Skyrim punch-shopping trick uses a save, a punch against a merchant, and a reload to refresh merchant stock. The follower-trainer exploit takes advantage of a different mismatch: training charges the player, but the gold can subsequently be recovered from the follower's inventory.
reddit.com
+1
4. Numerical feedback loops
Fortify Restoration. Duh, of course. 😏
In SGG, this could inspire a potion or enchantment loop where boosting one effect makes the next boost stronger, until values become absurd. The challenge is to make the discovery funny and rewarding, not merely a button that prints infinite money.
5. Physics and traversal
Horse-climbing mountains, bucket flying, butterfly flying, and the tilted-horse super-flight exploit.
This is a different playground altogether: movement constraints, collisions, force, and the difference between where the game thinks an object should be and where its physics actually sends it.
🧠 The idea tying all of this together
Here's the design principle I'd put at the top of our SGG notebook:
Every exploit begins with a rule. The discovery comes from finding a situation where two rules interact in an unexpected way.
A vendor has a visibility rule. A wall has a collision rule. A trainer has a payment rule. Alchemy has a magnitude rule. A horse has movement and terrain rules.
The player discovers that the rules don't quite agree with one another—and turns that disagreement into an advantage.
That gives us a wonderfully broad game concept. Spiffy's Gift can start with petty shop theft, then gradually introduce other systems and new ways to abuse them. Patches can close known loopholes while accidentally creating different ones.
We don't need to implement every exploit, and we certainly don't need to implement them all now. Some fit our current shop directly; others would inspire later locations or systems.
And here's the important distinction: we should recreate the principle behind each Skyrim exploit, not necessarily reproduce its exact button sequence or bug.
